WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.520
Something is wrong with the Chinese economy.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:11.080
You can see it at job fairs. In rows&nbsp;
of empty, half-finished apartments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:16.480
And in the conspicuous absence&nbsp;
of Chinese tourists everywhere.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:23.160
Take unemployment, for instance. In just&nbsp;
a few short years, the number of 16-24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.440
year olds who can’t find work has more&nbsp;
than doubled, from about 10% in 2019 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.760
more than twenty this summer, before the&nbsp;
government simply stopped publishing data.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.120
If you think the NASDAQ has&nbsp;
had a rough couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:46.800
just have a look at the Chinese stock market,&nbsp;
which has lost about 40% of its value since 2021.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.120
Or, how about real estate? Last&nbsp;
year, Chinese land sales reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.160
their lowest level this century.&nbsp;
One especially desperate developer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:04.360
is enticing new homebuyers with $70,000&nbsp;
worth of – I kid you not – gold bars.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:10.760
Consumption is down. Deflation has set in.&nbsp;
And for the first time in our lifetimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.760
its population has begun to shrink.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:21.400
The question is no longer when but whether&nbsp;
its economy will surpass America’s.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.120
For the past two decades, most agreed&nbsp;
that the “West is declining” and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:32.560
“East is rising”. But, increasingly,&nbsp;
it feels as though the East has risen.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.880
Are we witnessing Peak China?

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:43.720
Sponsored by Nebula, the exclusive home&nbsp;
to my Original series “China, Actually”.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:49.040
The 2008 Financial Crisis should’ve&nbsp;
been disastrous for China.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.160
The United States is its largest&nbsp;
trading partner. Thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.920
Chinese factories and millions of&nbsp;
workers rely on American consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.040
who suddenly found themselves out of work and&nbsp;
on the hook for mortgages they couldn’t afford.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.800
Yet, by two-thousand nine, China&nbsp;
was back on track. It was, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.960
the first major economy to recover. While&nbsp;
the U.S., Japan, Germany, and the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:23.680
the world contracted, China’s economy actually&nbsp;
grew – and by nearly double-digits, no less!

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.840
Ten years later, China was ground zero&nbsp;
for the COVID-19 pandemic. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:37.720
supply and demand were disrupted. Factory and&nbsp;
service workers had no ability to work from home.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:44.920
But, once again, Beijing defied expectations.&nbsp;
It not only weathered the storm but was – as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:53.520
before – the very first country to recover. Soon,&nbsp;
its exports soared to heights never-before-seen.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.160
In short: no one has ever won by&nbsp;
betting against the Chinese economy.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.120
So, how does China do it?

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:06.600
It’s simple: when in doubt, it builds.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:14.400
Roads. Bridges. Skyscrapers.&nbsp;
Stadiums. Airports. Whatever this is.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.600
It goes something like this:

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.560
First, Beijing makes borrowing&nbsp;
money laughably easy. It lowers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.560
interest rates and drowns state-owned&nbsp;
enterprises with generous subsidies.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:33.080
When money is free and losses socialized, you’d&nbsp;
have to be an idiot not to build highways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.920
bridges, and tunnels — even if they lead nowhere.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.040
Much of this investment, obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:47.920
is unproductive. But it creates jobs and&nbsp;
raises the country’s GDP just the same!

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.280
Now, for a long time, this whirlwind&nbsp;
of construction actually made sense.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.040
When Deng Xiaoping rose to power in the&nbsp;
1970s, China was poor, underdeveloped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.080
and in desperate need for even&nbsp;
the most basic infrastructure.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:09.680
The simplest two-lane road or single-story&nbsp;
school was a massive improvement from nothing.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.080
Just to catch up with the rich world, China&nbsp;
needed to build first and think later.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:24.400
In 2009, Al Jazeera ran a story about Ordos,&nbsp;
China’s eerie “ghost city” in the desert. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.600
in this empty Potemkin Village, it proclaimed,&nbsp;
was irrefutable evidence of a housing bubble.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.880
Today, Ordos is quiet but far from empty,&nbsp;
with about 2 million residents in total.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:44.480
More recently, in 2017, CNN published this image&nbsp;
with the headline “China’s metro station in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:51.280
middle of nowhere”. Readers around the world&nbsp;
laughed at this hilarious white elephant.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.160
Then, a mere two years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:59.120
the station had transformed into this&nbsp;
and laughter was replaced with awe.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.480
More often than not, critics were wrong and demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.560
eventually caught up with supply&nbsp;
as millions migrated to cities.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.600
And yet, this can’t go on forever.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:18.360
By definition, to maintain economic growth,&nbsp;
China has to keep investing more and more.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:24.360
Meanwhile, the returns on that&nbsp;
construction are getting worse and worse.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:31.080
This is China’s capital-to-output ratio –&nbsp;
essentially, how productive its investment.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.200
Twenty years ago, it took $4 worth of construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:40.200
to generate a one-dollar return.&nbsp;
Today, it takes about twelve.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:45.760
China is already a middle-income country&nbsp;
with rich country infrastructure.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.880
With 25,000 miles of high-speed&nbsp;
rail and 3 million miles of highway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.160
it’s simply running out of things to build.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.640
With nearly every obvious opportunity exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:02.320
China is resorting to increasingly&nbsp;
questionable projects.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.480
This, for example, is the&nbsp;
tallest bridge in the world.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.040
At 1,800 feet above the ground&nbsp;
and measuring 2,300 feet long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.840
it’s an architectural and engineering marvel.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:20.600
Impressive? Absolutely!&nbsp;
Economically-sound? Doubtful.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:24.320
It’s located not in Shanghai,&nbsp;
Chongqing, or Hangzhou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.920
but in China’s equivalent of rural Mississippi.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:35.240
Guizhou is the 4th poorest of the country’s&nbsp;
31 provinces and provincial-level cities.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:41.640
But its per-capita GDP of $7,700 hasn’t&nbsp;
stopped it from building about 500 new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:47.560
bridges in just two years – many of&nbsp;
which are among the world’s tallest.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:54.040
Now, perhaps this $150 million bridge in the&nbsp;
mountains will be the next Ordos or metro station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.040 --> 00:07:01.000
quote, “in the middle of nowhere”. Perhaps it will&nbsp;
spring to life and transform the local economy.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.040
Given China’s track record, it would&nbsp;
be foolish to bet against any one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:11.200
project. But it would also be foolish&nbsp;
to think this could go on indefinitely.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.600
Think about it like this: Taking on&nbsp;
debt is perfectly normal. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:21.920
that debt takes years to generate&nbsp;
returns – also perfectly fine. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:28.320
when debt continues growing faster than&nbsp;
GDP, we know there’s a serious problem.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.760
Previously, China would build, say, a new&nbsp;
airport – represented here by an increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:39.720
debt and it might sit empty for a time while&nbsp;
demand for flights caught up with supply. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:45.920
might read articles in the meantime about this&nbsp;
wasteful “ghost airport”. But, after a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:54.520
we’d see a corresponding rise in GDP&nbsp;
– represented here. So far, so good.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:08:01.080
Then, starting around 2009, we start&nbsp;
to see debt and GDP diverge. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:06.000
debt is still boosting GDP, but by a lot less.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.480
We can see this more clearly by dividing the debt&nbsp;
line by the GDP line. The two grow at pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:19.880
the same rate – that is, sustainably – until&nbsp;
2009, when debt starts growing much faster. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:26.960
that point, China keeps building more highways&nbsp;
and airports, but demand never catches up.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.960
This is the dilemma China&nbsp;
finds itself in today. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:36.400
old model – the only one it's&nbsp;
ever known – no longer works.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:42.960
Now, there are only four ways to increase&nbsp;
GDP. Investment, which, as we’ve already seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:50.360
isn’t a viable option. Government spending,&nbsp;
which is more-or-less a constant. And exports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:55.000
which the U.S. and other countries&nbsp;
are actively trying to reduce.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.040
That leaves only one route forward:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:05.560
consumption. In other words, China needs to&nbsp;
somehow convince people to spend more money.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:12.040
And, needless to say, gentle persuasion is not&nbsp;
something the Party has been known to excel at.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:18.960
As you can see, not only has it thus far failed to&nbsp;
do so, but it’s actually made negative progress.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:25.160
Chinese consumers spend less than 40%&nbsp;
of their GDP. Americans, by contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:33.680
spend nearly 70%, Europeans, over 50%,&nbsp;
and the average global consumer, 55.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.520
The main reason is real estate.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:40.920
Until recently, buying property&nbsp;
was the tried-and-true investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.040
strategy for middle-class households.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.160
Save prodigiously, pool together money&nbsp;
from family, and every urban couple could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:55.640
buy an apartment – or more often, two&nbsp;
or three – and live the Chinese Dream.

00:09:55.640 --> 00:10:00.240
Most of these homes were sold before&nbsp;
construction had even finished – allowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.360
developers to immediately take these&nbsp;
down payments and start building more.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.400
All it took, therefore, was one set of buyers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:14.240
stop making payments last year and&nbsp;
the whole system came crashing down.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:20.560
For two decades, the country was taught that&nbsp;
property was the only safe investment. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:27.880
property stopped being a safe investment.&nbsp;
No wonder Chinese consumers are worried.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:31.520
Now the problem is one of confidence.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.960
It’s simple, really: When&nbsp;
you receive your paycheck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.600
how do you decide whether to save or spend it?

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.360
Generally speaking, you spend&nbsp;
it when you’re confident you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.240
keep receiving more and you’ll&nbsp;
save it if you aren’t so sure.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.880
Well, Chinese consumers aren’t so sure.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:54.040
In the last 4 years, they’ve absolutely&nbsp;
stuffed their bank accounts to the brim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.040 --> 00:11:00.560
with money and cut back on big, flashy&nbsp;
purchases like cars, TVs, and furniture.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:07.760
They’re still traveling, but mostly domestically.&nbsp;
They’re still spending, but mostly on sales.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:14.480
So, naturally, in order to incentivize&nbsp;
purchases, companies are lowering their prices.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:21.120
These lower prices – and therefore profits&nbsp;
– are leading to layoffs and reduced wages.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.320
Finally, these layoffs are making&nbsp;
consumers even less confident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:30.920
and as a result, less likely to go&nbsp;
shopping – completing the cycle.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:34.920
Until and unless China interrupts&nbsp;
this positive feedback loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.560
it will be forced to accept lower economic growth.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:46.800
Now, alright, let’s put things in perspective.&nbsp;
Between 1979 and today, China saw what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:52.800
World Bank calls “the fastest sustained&nbsp;
expansion by a major economy in history”.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.280
Those four decades were anomalous. They weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:00.680
sustainable. And they were&nbsp;
simply never going to last.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:07.080
Its economy is still growing – albeit much&nbsp;
more slowly. Its middle class is still 700&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:13.200
million people strong. And its military&nbsp;
is still the world’s second-largest.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:18.560
China is not going away. To argue&nbsp;
otherwise is to overcorrect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:24.680
the prior era of optimism — to make the&nbsp;
same mistake, in the opposite direction.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.560
But the lightning-fast growth&nbsp;
that led to speculation about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:34.080
an “Asian Century” – is going away.&nbsp;
And there’s very little it can do.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.080
At the very heart of this&nbsp;
slowdown are demographics.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:43.080
As long as the population was growing,&nbsp;
China could build almost aimlessly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:49.760
confident there would be a steady supply of bodies&nbsp;
to fill every “ghost city”, airport, and highway.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:56.240
But, as of last year, China is now actively&nbsp;
shrinking. Its birth rate has already dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:03.560
to the lowest level in the world – just&nbsp;
1.09 children per woman. And by 2030,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:09.080
the labor force is expected to&nbsp;
decrease by about 1% every year.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:12.720
One of the great mysteries behind&nbsp;
this demographic collapse is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:19.000
China kept the One-Child Policy&nbsp;
around for so long – until 2016.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:24.400
Surely, its leaders had to have known&nbsp;
the population would begin to shrink. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:28.480
why then were families still&nbsp;
prohibited from having more children?

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:32.080
Find out the answer to this&nbsp;
question in the latest, 20-minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

